<section class="hero compact">
    <p class="eyebrow">EMERALD / PACKAGE DATABASE</p>
    <h1>pkgadd</h1>

    <p class="lead">Install a built Vylen package archive into the target root and register its files in the package database.</p>
    <div class="command-line"># pkgadd [options] &lt;package&gt;</div>
</section>

<section class="doc-grid">
    <aside class="toc">
        <p class="toc-title">pkgadd</p>

        <a href="#/pm/">Package manuals</a>
        <a href="#/pm/pkgadd#usage">Usage</a>
        <a href="#/pm/pkgadd#workflow">Workflow</a>
        <a href="#/pm/pkgadd#safety">Safety</a>
    </aside>

    <article class="doc">
        <h2 id="usage">Usage</h2>

        <pre><code>pkgadd &lt;package-file&gt;
pkgadd -f &lt;package-file&gt;
pkgadd --root /mnt &lt;package-file&gt;
pkgadd --help</code></pre>

        <p>
            The exact archive format is produced by the Vylen build toolchain.
            Use <code>pkgadd</code> for a package that has already passed its
            build and integrity checks. Emerald normally calls it for you during
            <code>emerald install</code>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="workflow">Installation workflow</h2>

        <ol>
            <li>Validate that the archive is readable and matches the expected package metadata.</li>
            <li>Resolve conflicts and required dependencies before changing the filesystem.</li>
            <li>Extract files into the target root.</li>
            <li>Record ownership, version, file list, and configuration backups.</li>
            <li>Run package triggers required by the installed software.</li>
        </ol>

        <pre><code>emerald install vivaldi
# equivalent low-level phase, after building:
pkgadd /var/cache/emerald/packages/vivaldi-8.2.4133.52-1.epkg.tar.xz</code></pre>

        <h2 id="safety">Safety notes</h2>

        <p>
            Do not use <code>pkgadd</code> to bypass dependency resolution on a
            running system unless you understand every dependency and trigger
            involved. For normal system changes prefer Emerald. Inspect the
            archive and test in a temporary root when developing a new port.
        </p>
    </article>
</section>
